WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.790
- [Narrator] This Great
Big Story was made possible

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:03.200
by Wells Fargo.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.860
Established 1852.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.913
Re-established 2018.

00:00:06.913 --> 00:00:09.290
(elegant music)

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.700
- [Mitali] The water we
swim in is dangerous,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.173
and someone has died in it.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.980
Growing up, I had people tell me

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.880
that the water was so dangerous

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.500
that there were three
prisoners who didn't make it.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.290
And the reason Alcatraz was there

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.470
was because they had sharks in the water,

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.260
and you can't swim at all,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.053
but now I know that's not true.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:38.663
I'm Mitali.

00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:40.620
I'm 16, and I've swum from Alcatraz

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.480
to San Francisco 48 times.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.313
- I'm Anaya.

00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:46.230
I'm 12, and I've done the
Alcatraz swim 38 times,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.180
and Mitali's my sister.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.020
- I used to be the youngest person

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.270
to swim the span of
the Golden Gate Bridge,

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.373
but my sister holds that world record now.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.720
The first time I swam Alcatraz
was when I was 10 years old,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.063
and I was inspired by my friends
who had swum it before me.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.990
We train once a week every Sunday

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:13.340
with our open water swim team.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.790
From just Alcatraz to the beach,

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:18.680
that takes me around 35 to 40 minutes,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.980
but at my fastest time, it's 27 minutes.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.446
- [Man] Guys, wanna know
the water temperature?

00:01:23.446 --> 00:01:27.880
57.5 right now.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.410
- [Anaya] That water is freezing,

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.773
but you feel so good because
you're in the open ocean.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.320
The water's murky, so you
can't really see anything,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.110
but you can feel the water,

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:40.770
and you feel like you're
on top of the world,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.763
but you're in the middle of the ocean.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.760
It's hard to have that mental
strength that you can do this

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.750
because it's just so dangerous
and unusual to do it.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.360
- [Mitali] And so whenever we swim,

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.190
when I take a breath
and she takes a breath,

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.940
we end up looking at each other,

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.730
and it's really comforting
to see that there's someone

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.773
out there with you while you're swimming.

00:02:01.514 --> 00:02:02.889
- [Woman] Good job, children!

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.940
(gentle music)

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.880
- [Mitali] People are
generally very shocked.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.840
That's the first response
because it's something

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.173
that no one's ever
really thought of doing.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:15.910
- [Anaya] I grew up with this,

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:20.630
so I never knew a world where
it was unusual to do this.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.830
If other kids wanna do
this, I encourage it.

